00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.410
ANDRE: I'm Andre Kelly.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.650
I'm the product manager
for Stackdriver IRM.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.933
IRM stands for incident
response and management.

00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:00.100
AJA: So can you tell us a
little bit more about IRM?

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.230
I've heard a lot about it,
and I've heard a lot about it,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.230
actually, internally at Google.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.540
But I don't know
if external folks

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.740
have heard much about it yet.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.240
ANDRE: Sure.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.010
So IRM's vision is kind of
to provide fewer, shorter,

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.480
and smaller outages.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.520
And the way we try to do
that is through context

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.900
and continuous learning--

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.420
one, to bring as much
context to the user

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.300
during a response, and then
two, capturing that response

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.630
information so future outages
of similar types are shorter.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.658
So it's natural,
continuous learning.

00:01:24.658 --> 00:01:26.200
JON: How would
developers and anybody

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.940
using this be able
to access this?

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:29.440
ANDRE: Sure.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.160
So that's a great question.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.390
Currently, externally
we're available in alpha.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.655
So you can go to
cloud.google.com/instant

00:01:34.655 --> 00:01:38.290
response, and you'll see a
page where you can sign up

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:39.077
for the alpha.

00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:41.410
AJA: I heard that we're using
this internally at Google.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.250
Is that true?

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:42.370
ANDRE: Yeah.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.240
That's a good point I think
I'll expound on a little bit.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.670
So you should know that
IRM started internally,

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.880
and it's really the next
generation Google incident

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.232
response set of tools.

00:01:50.232 --> 00:01:51.940
It combines a lot of
known best practices

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.940
internal to Google, a lot
of previous tools that

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.245
were very disparate, that
limited our data analytics

00:01:56.245 --> 00:01:57.970
and capabilities
around incidence.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.740
So you combine it
all together in IRM,

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.260
learning from those best
practices and new ideas

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.860
about data management.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.430
How do we leverage the
ops data to help the user

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.110
in the investigation process?

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.150
That relates to--

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.740
SRE is using it internally,
and our dev on call's

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.100
also using it internally.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.280
JON: How can developers
interacting with it--

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.590
suppose that there are
any issues that may arise.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.590
How can they submit
feedback with this?

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.090
We're always big on getting our
feedback from our developers

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.173
and making things greater.

00:02:23.173 --> 00:02:23.710
ANDRE: Yes.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:24.310
Totally agree.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.450
And being the alpha
product, we're

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.867
looking for that
external feedback

00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:28.110
to make the product better.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.860
So in the app in the
top right-hand corner

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.277
and all stacked
over apps, there's

00:02:31.277 --> 00:02:32.510
a button to submit feedback.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:33.887
We also have that mechanism.

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:35.470
And that mechanism
takes a screenshot.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.047
So if there's something
hokey going on in the UI,

00:02:38.047 --> 00:02:39.880
it's a great way to
submit that information.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.533
In our docks, there's also
email addresses for contact

00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:44.950
us that go straight to the
leadership of the team in terms

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.840
of issues you're
having or if something

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.282
seems down, for example.

00:02:48.282 --> 00:02:49.990
AJA: I know I'm getting
a little bit away

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.180
from the specific product,
but can you tell us

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.150
some about SRE philosophies and
how you've brought that stuff

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.630
into the Stackdriver tools?

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:57.130
ANDRE: Sure.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.255
There's about four of them.

00:02:58.255 --> 00:03:02.260
But one of the key ones we hone
in on is make tomorrow better.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.350
It's just an idea of
doing some work today

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.440
so tomorrow this kind
of work is easier to do.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.820
So it's a lot of
ways to go with it.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.320
So for IRM, that's the
continuous learning.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.833
Overall, this is also
about reducing toil.

00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:17.500
So things that are
very mundane that you

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.900
do over and over again, how
can we make it automated

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.145
so robots do that?

00:03:21.145 --> 00:03:23.020
And that's one of the
key things where we say

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.850
we bring the context to you.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.490
So it was very common
in engineers' minds

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.652
to go look at the
chart of the metric,

00:03:29.652 --> 00:03:31.360
because no one really
believes the alert.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.520
You have to see it for yourself
to affirm that something

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.270
is actually wrong.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.025
I don't want to step
away for no reason.

00:03:36.025 --> 00:03:37.400
And so we just
bring that to you.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.300
So as soon as you respond, you
see that information and things

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.440
that are really related to it.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.240
So that's part of
a way we're making

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.307
SRE tenants come to life.

00:03:46.307 --> 00:03:48.640
AJA: So you are acknowledging
the fact that we are human

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.057
and we have foibles,
and making it

00:03:50.057 --> 00:03:52.325
so that the tooling works
with those trends as opposed

00:03:52.325 --> 00:03:53.200
to fights against us.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.260
ANDRE: That's exactly right.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.093
AJA: That's awesome.

00:03:55.093 --> 00:03:57.520
ANDRE: We have to recognize
humans are humans.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.858
JON: What would you
say is the gem of SRE?

00:03:59.858 --> 00:04:01.900
Which practice do you
believe will be the better?

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.300
ANDRE: I think the
gem of SRE is actually

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.680
one of the harder parts of it.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.753
It's the culture kind
of reflected in SRE.

00:04:08.753 --> 00:04:10.420
But one of the key
things in our culture

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:14.170
is a contract, the contract
between business developers

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.720
and operators, that we really
believe in this error budget.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.890
And we're going to
believe in SLOs.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.880
And from that,
developers and business

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.980
say, OK, so we know
we can push changes

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.050
this fast to be
competitive in the market.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.120
And we know that, if in doing
those changes something breaks,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.140
we'll know we'll stop
because reliability

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.760
is of the utmost importance.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.060
And that's part of
the op's charter.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.750
Make sure the
application's available.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.090
Make sure that your
users are happy.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.550
But it requires a
triad to really agree

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.160
on what that means, what
the guardrails of that kind

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.330
of responsibility is.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.580
And I think that's one of
the key things about the SRE

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.317
culture that I think is key
to bringing external as well.

00:04:51.317 --> 00:04:52.150
AJA: That's awesome.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.012
So we're actually
already out of time.

00:04:54.012 --> 00:04:56.470
Is there anything that you
would like the listeners to know

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.053
or a place that they
can find out more

00:04:58.053 --> 00:05:00.290
about you or about the
tools you're working on?

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:03.190
ANDRE: So IRM is the
tool I'm working on.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.013
As I mentioned, we're
in alpha, and we'll

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:06.430
plan to do a lot
more development.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:07.220
Looking forward
to feedback there.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.800
So please sign up for the alpha.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:09.820
Other things I'm
looking forward to

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:11.530
is just kind of how
Stackdriver continues

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.798
to provide kind of internal
tooling and skill sets

00:05:14.798 --> 00:05:16.090
externally through our tooling.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.180
One of the great examples
of that is Profiler.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.160
It just went GA today.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.830
So we're looking forward to
building up on that foundation.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:23.210
AJA: That's awesome.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.600
Well, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.433
We hope to have you
back in a couple months.

00:05:26.433 --> 00:05:29.210
And we can talk at length
about IRM and incident response

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.440
and SRE best practices.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.050
ANDRE: That sounds great.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:31.967
Looking forward to it.

00:05:31.967 --> 00:05:33.800
Thank you.

