00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.762
we have a shared responsibility
model helping people understand

00:06:32.762 --> 00:06:34.220
here's what you're
responsible for,

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.522
here's what GKE security
products do for you.

00:06:36.522 --> 00:06:37.980
AJA: I think I
understood all that.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.397
MARK: What are we showcasing
or talking about here at Next

00:06:40.397 --> 00:06:41.710
around those topics?

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:42.320
ANNE: Yes.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.340
Oh, about container
security specifically,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.360
we have the GA of binary
authorization coming soon,

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:48.890
which is very exciting.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.620
So binary authorization
is deploy time controls

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.930
so that you make sure that
you only deploy container

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:55.280
images that you trust.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.285
So you can decide what
those ATA stations are.

00:06:57.285 --> 00:06:59.660
So you know-- I want to make
sure this image got scanned.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.730
I want to make sure that my
QA team took a peek at it.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:02.660
I want to make sure--

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.125
whatever guardrails
your organization needs,

00:07:05.125 --> 00:07:06.500
that's going in
your CICD system.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.290
So it's all about that kind of
shifting left mentality rather

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.292
than security comes
at the end, security

00:07:11.292 --> 00:07:12.500
goes through the whole thing.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.870
And we make sure that you're
deploying what you trust.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:15.920
That's an exciting one.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.900
We also today, this
morning, announced

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.510
the beta for GKE
Sandbox is coming soon.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:24.530
And that one is-- if you heard
gVisor last year in Copenhagen,

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.360
Google opensource gVisor--

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.610
container isolation
based on a user space

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.295
kernel that filters sys calls.

00:07:30.295 --> 00:07:32.045
Well, a little more
complicated than that,

00:07:32.045 --> 00:07:33.200
but that's a nice way to put it.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.200
I think the gVisor team
would be cranky with me.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.050
But basically thinking about
how can that user space

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.660
kernal handle sys calls so it
doesn't get down to the host,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.400
and we kind reduce
the tax service

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.870
for the host kernel
in a container setup.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.860
So we now have a productized
version of that in GKE.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.070
So with one click, you
can get GKE Sandbox.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.238
AJA: Oh, that's super cool.

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.780
It sounds like the
Trust and Security

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.810
Team is trying to make tooling
so that people can actually

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:59.320
think about this from start
to finish as opposed to just,

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.420
oh, right, that security thing,
we should probably do that.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:03.623
Is that the whole purpose?

00:08:03.623 --> 00:08:05.540
ANNE: Yes, that's a big
part of our philosophy

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:08.630
is that we have this phrase
of security by default.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:10.760
And it's really
that, out of the box,

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.320
you should not be able to
get yourself into trouble.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.065
There's always
endless configurations

00:08:15.065 --> 00:08:16.190
that endless customization.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:20.005
But if I just say go, I want
to make Kubernetes dashboards

00:08:20.005 --> 00:08:20.630
aren't exposed.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.755
I want to make sure my
credentials are locked down.

00:08:22.755 --> 00:08:24.485
Those things should
come out of the box.

00:08:24.485 --> 00:08:26.090
MARK: Are there any
sessions happening

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:27.380
around any of these topics?

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:28.250
ANNE: Absolutely.

