00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:48.730
ANNE: Thank you for having me.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.960
BRIAN: Hey, we're here on
day two with Brian, Mark--

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:53.720
MARK: Yep.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:54.320
BRIAN: --and?

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:54.800
MARIO: Mario.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:55.300
Hi.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:00.010
I'm a GDG organizer from Munich,
and we run the cloud community

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:00.710
there.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:02.190
Yeah, I'm happy to be here.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:02.773
MARK: Awesome.

00:10:02.773 --> 00:10:03.890
Well, welcome to Next.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:05.490
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:07.910
What's been a highlight for
you so far here at Next?

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:10.280
MARIO: So basically
two highlights.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.040
The first highlight, of
course, was Anthos yesterday,

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.830
but bigger impact
even more is for me

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:19.610
and for a lot of our customers
is MS SQL as a managed

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:21.920
database in Google Cloud.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:22.420
BRIAN: Neat.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:23.325
And so I was excited
about that, too,

00:10:23.325 --> 00:10:25.950
but why is that interesting
to you and your people?

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:29.090
MARIO: In Germany, a lot of
companies still use MS SQL

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.040
and have big MS SQL clusters.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.290
And moving to Cloud is
hard for German companies

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.930
due to regulations
of GDPR and privacy.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:41.180
And big companies
don't trust vendors,

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.080
and they want to have
it all their method.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:43.622
BRIAN: Right.

00:10:43.622 --> 00:10:47.780
MARIO: But they start to see
that maintaining infrastructure

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.800
is expensive, and
it's hard to do.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.730
And they want to
move to the Cloud.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.550
And when you provide
managed service

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:58.250
like MS SQL, which is your
database that you used

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:02.510
for, like, 10, 15 years, it
makes them way more comfortable

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:05.293
to take the step and
move into the Cloud.

00:11:05.293 --> 00:11:05.960
MARK: Fantastic.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.030
Have you seen anything in
particular on the show floor

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:10.040
that you've also thought was
particularly interesting?

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.540
MARIO: Yeah, I
talked to the people

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:15.590
from MongoDB and from
Elastic, and it's cool

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:20.240
that Google tries to team up
with the different databases

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.270
and search engines and all
of the open source projects

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.960
that are basically out there and
try to move it into the Cloud

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.030
as well as you don't
need another service

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:31.072
and another service and
another service provider.

00:11:31.072 --> 00:11:33.680
You can just have everything
under one company.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.270
BRIAN: All connected closely.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.790
Synergy.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:39.290
Is there anything
you'd like to make

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.770
sure more people knew about your
work or Cloud or other things?

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:43.940
MARIO: Actually, there is.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.300
So we did a lot of study
gems in the last month

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.620
to get people
involved into what is

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:51.870
cloud, what is infrastructure.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.370
And when you do a
show of hands, you

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.910
have, like, a room of
40 people, 50 people.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:58.400
And you ask them, yeah
do you use containers?

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.620
And you get all of them
showing their hands.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.160
And then you ask something
like, do you use Kubernetes?

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:07.040
And you have, like, three people
in the room showing hands.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.160
And what the industry
thinks is standard

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.530
or what many people say
is standard-- like, yeah,

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:14.226
all of the people
use Kubernetes--

00:12:14.226 --> 00:12:15.780
it is not the case.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:18.440
So there are a lot of
companies, especially

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:21.080
that are not so big, like
not of the big players,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:24.140
but still have a lot
of systems going on.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.240
And they don't know
about the technology

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.820
or they are afraid of moving
the technology they have.

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.830
Because we did it
for 10 years, and we

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:32.540
will continue the
way how we do it.

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.570
And when you show them
things like Kubernetes

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.700
or you show them a
service measure of Istio

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:41.030
where they can integrate their
bare metal machines combined

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.550
with remote hosted
machines, they love it.

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:44.940
But they don't know about it.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.750
And I think what
is our job to do

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.270
as communities or
experts in this field

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.700
is approach the people,
show them there are cool

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.550
new ways that make
your job easier.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:59.120
And we provide free trainings,
like the Google community does

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:01.820
with Qwiklabs, to
just teach people

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:02.990
there are cool, new ways.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:04.370
There are cool,
new technologies,

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:05.900
and they are not hard to learn.

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.630
But we just have to go
out and get the people

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.353
to do it, because it's
not a thing about, yeah,

00:13:11.353 --> 00:13:12.770
it's only working
in Google Cloud,

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.810
because everything
is open source.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.277
And you can do it and
use it whenever you want.

00:13:17.277 --> 00:13:18.860
And that's what's
really, really cool.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.030
MARK: You mentioned
Qwiklabs, but are there

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:22.670
any other particular resources
that you would recommend

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:26.487
for people who are looking
to level up, so to speak,

00:13:26.487 --> 00:13:27.320
on things like that?

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:30.560
MARIO: There's actually a
really, really nice open source

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:34.170
platform for blogging,
which is dev.to,

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.710
and it's really
growing community.

00:13:36.710 --> 00:13:39.560
And you have lot of people
there that are just starting

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:41.190
getting into technology.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:45.140
And there are also a lot of
meta-articles for diversity

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:48.740
and getting involved
in tech communities.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:50.900
And there are a lot
of free resources

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:55.850
where you can find, like, how
do I use Docker in two articles

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.180
series and stuff like this.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.910
And I think this
is the resources

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:00.440
that people should check out.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:01.065
BRIAN: Awesome.

00:14:01.065 --> 00:14:01.702
Dev.to.

00:14:01.702 --> 00:14:03.410
Thank you very much
for coming and taking

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:04.180
time to chat with us.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.805
MARIO: Yeah, thank
you for inviting me.

00:14:05.805 --> 00:14:07.465
MARK: Thank you.

00:14:07.465 --> 00:14:09.710
So super happy here at
the Next chair floor

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:12.740
to have Valentin
Deleplace joining us today

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:13.730
to chat about Go stuff.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:14.820
How you doing, Valentin?

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:15.612
VALENTIN: I'm fine.

00:14:15.612 --> 00:14:17.450
Thanks for having
me, Mark and Jon.

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:19.560
JON: Me and Valentin are
very good friends, so--

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:20.353
VALENTIN: Oh yes.

00:14:20.353 --> 00:14:21.020
MARK: Excellent.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.770
Then you can just chat,
and I'll sit back.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:23.500
VALENTIN: Yeah.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.080
MARK: So, Valentin, I believe
you're speaking here at Next?

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:27.080
VALENTIN: Oh, yes, I am.

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:27.800
That's tomorrow.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.300
MARK: So what are
you talking about?

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.100
VALENTIN: I'm talking
about performance,

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.320
and more specifically,
performance

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.770
in Go in the public Cloud,
and more specifically, let's

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:38.060
say, GCP.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.880
JON: Pretty sure the gophers
are going crazy right now.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.030
Can we look forward to possibly
seeing you on livestream

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.782
so that our external
viewers can see you?

00:14:45.782 --> 00:14:46.490
VALENTIN: Oh yes.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.950
I just got very
confidential information

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.590
that's confirmed that
I will be livestreamed.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:52.140
MARK: Nice.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:52.340
That's good.

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:54.500
That should mean as well, if
you're listening to this later,

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.100
it'll be up on
YouTube pretty fast.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:56.720
JON: Exactly.

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:57.850
VALENTIN: Oh, good to know.

00:14:57.850 --> 00:14:58.100
MARK: Yeah.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:14:59.450
So what sort of stuff
are you covering

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:00.530
when you say performance?

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.125
That seems like
a very big topic.

00:15:02.125 --> 00:15:03.250
What does that mean to you?

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:06.330
VALENTIN: So performance
is making things faster,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:09.020
especially when things
are a little bit too slow.

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:13.250
And my talk is part high level,
because it's not specific to Go

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.320
and it's not specific
to the Cloud.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:18.790
It's sometimes about
saving cost and handling

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.280
more scalability, et cetera.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.330
But that's boring.

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:26.530
What I'm really talking about is
about improving user experience

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:29.260
by making everything
fast, snappy.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:30.280
That's the main point.

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:33.520
Because we care about
humans, not about machines.

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:34.270
JON: That's great.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:36.970
And can you give us a
sneak peek as to what

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:40.030
you would consider one of
the more optimal solutions

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:42.460
to making things
faster and quicker?

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:43.300
MARK: Hot tip.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:44.945
VALENTIN: I'll give
you the answer.

00:15:44.945 --> 00:15:45.445
It depends.

00:15:45.445 --> 00:15:46.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:48.295 --> 00:15:50.950
No, so usually the
most important thing

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:54.610
is not to have a lot of
prejudice about what is fast,

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:56.710
what is a good user experience.

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:59.740
You really have to measure
to find the bottleneck,

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:02.930
and oftentimes it's not
where you think it is.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.270
And once you have the
bottleneck, you work on that,

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:09.822
and very often it's possible
or even easy to fix that.

00:16:09.822 --> 00:16:12.280
MARK: What are your favorite
tools for tracking performance

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:13.660
metrics or things like that?

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:15.200
Like, how do you observe this?

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:18.070
VALENTIN: So I love
the benchmarking tool

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.140
integrated in the Go Tool link.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:21.530
That's really great.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:25.600
And then I really, really
love trace and ppov.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.220
It's plenty mainstream.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:30.060
I mean, many good
developers already use this.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.170
And if you're not sure what
the execution tracer is

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:36.970
or what ppov is, then you should
definitely come to my talk

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:37.870
tomorrow.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:38.560
MARK: Excellent.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.643
What's been your favorite
announcement so far here

00:16:40.643 --> 00:16:41.380
at Next?

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:45.880
VALENTIN: Well, I think we can
say that Cloud Run is huge.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:49.690
I mean, serverless plus
containers, that is so great.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:51.197
Thank you, Google.

00:16:51.197 --> 00:16:52.780
MARK: Coming back
to your talk, what's

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.150
the biggest challenge
you see people facing in

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:58.310
regards to performance, in
regards to their applications?

00:16:58.310 --> 00:17:00.310
VALENTIN: Well, that's
a great question.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:03.310
Usually it's a lack
of observability,

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:04.869
because it's always two parts.

00:17:04.869 --> 00:17:07.650
First you measure, and
then you fix things.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.630
And observing what is
slow and what is fast

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:12.609
is not as obvious as it sounds.

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:13.960
So there is tooling.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:16.990
Sometimes it's well
integrated, and sometimes it's

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:19.010
a little bit more challenging.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:22.510
And what I witness is as soon
as we've done the first part

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:26.160
of the work-- that is, clear
observability with a nice

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.480
waterfall view or
things like that--

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:32.590
then it becomes much more easy
to make the program faster.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:34.240
JON: Last question
before we let you

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:37.070
go is, who would you say your
talk is mainly geared towards?

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.070
I know you said it's more
of an experience talk,

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:40.750
but we'll let our
viewers know exactly

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.690
who should follow along.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.890
VALENTIN: Yes, so
the great thing

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:48.040
about Cloud and virtual
machines and serverless,

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.560
et cetera, that
Cloud then becomes

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:53.570
more and more decoupled for
the choice of your language.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:57.100
So most of them, you can
choose any popular language

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:57.950
and it will work.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:01.360
So some folks are using
more and more public clouds

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:03.730
and are wondering,
should I use Go?

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:04.870
Is it a good idea?

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:08.490
Will my team of developers
use that and like that?

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.870
And the key message
that I'm saying

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.620
is that Go is really
strong and good choice

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:16.660
for cloud computing.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:18.510
And I just don't say it.

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.460
I also demonstrate it.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:21.460
JON: Well, that's great.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:23.710
Well, Valentin, thank you
for joining us, and really,

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:25.210
really looking
forward to your talk.

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:25.960
VALENTIN: Thank you, Jon.

